    "x.kpi.savings": "This is what you could save in a year by making five concrete changes, such as emptying machines fuller and sorting more. It is an estimate from demo data and example prices. Every change is advice, and you decide.",
    "x.kpi.total_cost": "This is what waste management cost you over the year, after the credit you earn from cardboard and plastics. A pickup costs the same whether the machine is full or half-empty, so fuller pickups and less mixed waste bring it down.",
    "x.kpi.eur_per_t": "This is what you pay on average for every tonne of waste taken away, all waste types and material credits included. SMART compares it with similar machines across the whole Europress fleet. If yours is higher, you may be overpaying.",
    "x.kpi.recycling_rate": "This is the share of your waste, by weight, that is sorted instead of thrown into mixed waste. Mixed waste is the most expensive stream and it is burned, while cardboard and plastics earn a credit. More sorting means lower costs.",
    "x.kpi.tonnes": "This is how much waste the trucks took away from your machines. The less mixed waste in it, the less you pay. Weights are indicative, not official weighing.",
    "x.kpi.lifts": "This is how many times a truck emptied your machines. A pickup costs the same whether the machine is full or half-empty, so fewer and fuller pickups save money. SMART orders the truck automatically at the order level.",
    "x.kpi.avg_fill": "On average, this is how full your machines are when the truck empties them. The target is 85 %, because a pickup costs the same full or half-empty. The closer to 85 % you empty, the fewer trucks you pay for.",
    "x.kpi.co2": "This is the CO₂ from the trucks that empty your machines, estimated from the number of trips and the distance of each one. Fuller pickups mean fewer trips, so both emissions and costs go down.",
    "x.lever.early_lifts": "A pickup costs the same whether the machine is full or half-empty. When machines are emptied too early, you pay for trips you did not need. Setting SMART's order level to 85 % means fewer pickups for the same waste.",
    "x.lever.avoidable_fees": "These are charges you can avoid: wasted trips, rush orders and waste left beside the machine. Order on time and keep side waste off the site, and you stop paying them.",
    "x.lever.sorting": "Mixed waste is your most expensive stream and it is burned. Much of it is cardboard and plastic that could be recycled, and those even earn you a credit. Moving 20 % of your mixed waste to recycling cuts the bill.",
    "x.lever.tender": "When you pay more per tonne than the typical price across the Europress fleet, the difference is money you could save. Use the fleet price as your benchmark when the contract renews: ask your operator to match it, or put it out to tender.",
    "x.lever.invoice_check": "Some invoice lines are priced differently from your contract price list. This is not proof of a mistake, because side waste or combined loads can explain a difference. Check the lines and ask your operator to explain or correct them. The billing lines come from your waste operator, who shares them with Europress — you do not need to send invoices.",
    "x.prob.recycling_down": "A smaller share of your waste was sorted in the last three months than in the three before. That means more went into mixed waste, which is the most expensive stream and is burned.",
    "x.prob.cost_per_t_up": "Each tonne of waste cost you more in the last three months than in the three before. The reason can be more mixed waste, half-empty pickups, extra fees or a price change, so it is worth finding out which one.",
    "x.prob.half_empty": "Your machines were emptied less full than before. A pickup costs the same whether the machine is full or half-empty, so every half-empty pickup is money wasted. SMART orders the truck at the order level, and 85 % full is the target.",
    "x.prob.fees_up": "Extra fees grew compared with the three months before. These are charges you can avoid, such as wasted trips, rush orders and waste left beside the machine. Each one is money spent for nothing.",
